#title()

I once had to roleplay _Very Senior Leader_ in a communication skills training
session. The guy who was practising on me delivered about 2 minutes of spiel on
obscure-technical-subject and the first time he paused for breath I asked "And
what do you want me to _do_?". That was the point his wheels came off.

One of the presentation styles you see from techy types is the story.
It opens, very logically, with a problem statement and then builds through careful
argument to a conclusion. A good story will detail analyses, why they were done, what
gets learnt from each, the limitations thereof. It's all very scientific, clear, and
rational, and would make an excellent (although maybe slightly dull) academic lecture.

And it's useless. The only people in the company who have the technical skills
to understand it are your immediate team. All your Colleagues From The Other Functions do not
have --- and should not have to have --- those skills. What they and the Very Senior Leaders care
about is _the next decision_. All your technical cleverness is --- best case --- white noise that
obscures the actual message.

Fortunately, there is a simple way to fix this, but it begins by putting your pride on the
shelf and realising that no-one cares about the technical details of your work. I state this
deliberately harshly; this is not a free pass to do a bad job on the analysis. It just
means that your Colleagues From The Other Functions shouldn't have to check your working
for you in a 100-slide deck#footnote[Yes, I know that technical review exists, and yes, I know that
is a different style of presentation. But most of your presentations aren't that.].

Think about these things:

+ What decision are you trying to impact? Why does your work matter?
+ What is your recommendation? What are the risks? What are the alternatives? If you can't tell
  from the data, what would it cost to find out?
+ What are the key analyses that support the narrative around decision / risks /
  alternatives? How are they interpreted?

Note also that if you can't answer these questions without too much thought you have problems
with your project that you need to fix immediately. You don't know why you're doing what
you're doing and you therefore don't know that what you're doing is the
Right Thing for The Business.

But those points (and in roughly that order) are how you should structure your
presentation#footnote[Don't, by the way, treat the backup material as a dumping ground for
toxic waste. Every slide in the deck --- even in backup material --- needs to earn its place.]. The
how-you-got-there story style is useful as a history lesson and as nothing else#footnote[If
you want to impress the world with your technical cleverness write a blog.].
Getting your presentation right requires you to step outside your comfort zone, talk to the Colleagues
From The Other Functions throughout the whole project, and collaboratively build a
narrative. You still need a _story_. It's just
not the one you thought it was. And sometimes the story isn't one The Business wants to hear.
You've still got to tell it, though.

You are not a data scientist in a box and you are not a graph-plotting machine for the Other
Functions#footnote[I have heard "I do all these analyses that Someone summarises onto
one slide that I don't even present" any number of times. The key cure for this pathology
is to stop functioning as a high-level programming language for your Colleagues From The Other Functions
and instead to focus on the business context of what you do.]. You are not there to perform your
favourite complicated analysis. You are there to help The Business
get from data to decision.
